"texte": [
"Journalistic Column — Across bustling street markets from Lagos and Lima to Jakarta and Naples, millions of vendors, artisans, domestic workers, transport operators, and small-scale recyclers sustain urban life outside formal legal registration, labor contracts, and tax registries. According to the International Labour Organization, more than sixty percent of the world's employed population—nearly two billion workers—earn their livelihoods in the informal economy. Long dismissed by mid-twentieth-century modernization theorists as a temporary residue of underdevelopment destined to vanish as formal factories expanded, informality has instead proven remarkably durable, embodying deep structural contradictions at the heart of global capitalism.",
"On one hand, the informal sector operates as an indispensable shock absorber and engine of grassroots ingenuity. In economies where formal job creation lags far behind demographic growth, informal enterprise provides immediate subsistence, flexible entry without bureaucratic hurdles, and affordable goods and services tailored to low-income households. From neighborhood repair workshops and street-food kitchens to community savings associations, informal networks demonstrate extraordinary adaptability, social solidarity, and entrepreneurial resilience during economic downturns when formal corporations lay off staff.",
"On the other hand, celebrating informality uncritically obscures the severe vulnerabilities it imposes on workers and public institutions alike. Deprived of legal recognition, informal workers typically lack minimum-wage guarantees, workplace safety protections, paid sick leave, maternity benefits, and old-age pensions. Unable to present formal collateral or audited accounts, micro-entrepreneurs are routinely shut out of affordable bank credit, forcing them to rely on predatory informal moneylenders whose exorbitant interest rates trap businesses in low-productivity survival. Simultaneously, weak tax collection from vast unregistered sectors starves states of the fiscal revenue needed to fund public schools, clinics, and infrastructure—perpetuating a vicious cycle of poor public services and low civic trust.",
"Furthermore, the boundary between the formal and informal spheres is far more porous than conventional statistics suggest. Many registered corporations quietly rely on unregistered subcontractors, informal home-based seamstresses, and off-the-books delivery couriers to minimize labor costs and evade regulatory compliance. Informality is therefore not an isolated parallel universe; it is frequently integrated into formal supply chains as a structural mechanism for externalizing economic risk onto the most precarious workers.",
"Addressing the contradictions of the informal economy therefore requires moving beyond punitive crackdowns that merely criminalize street vendors without offering viable alternatives. Effective policy must focus on progressive formalization through positive incentives: simplifying business registration, extending portable social insurance to self-employed workers, expanding microfinance and cooperative credit, and holding formal firms accountable for labor standards across their entire supply chains. Only by combining protection with opportunity can societies transform informal survival into dignified, productive work."
