"In-Depth Interview — Conversation with infectious-disease epidemiologist Dr. Tariq Al-Mansoor — Journalist: Over the past century, vaccines, antibiotics, antiviral therapies, and genomic sequencing have achieved extraordinary victories over infectious pathogens. Yet recent outbreaks of novel viral and zoonotic diseases have caught even advanced healthcare systems off guard. Why does modern medicine remain structurally vulnerable to emerging pathogens?",
"Dr. Tariq Al-Mansoor: Because medical science is frequently forced into a reactive posture against ecological and evolutionary forces moving faster than clinical pipelines. Most emerging human pathogens—roughly seventy percent—are zoonotic, jumping from wildlife or livestock into human populations. Deforestation, intensive factory farming, wildlife trade, and rapid urbanization continuously bring humans into closer contact with novel animal viruses, while global air travel enables a localized spillover to cross continents within twenty-four hours. Even with miraculous breakthroughs like mRNA vaccine platforms, developing, testing, manufacturing, and distributing countermeasures globally inevitably lags behind exponential viral transmission.",
"Journalist: Beyond biological speed, what institutional and economic weaknesses limit our medical response?",
"Dr. Tariq Al-Mansoor: Market incentives in pharmaceutical research are poorly aligned with epidemic preparedness. Developing vaccines or broad-spectrum antivirals against pathogens that might not emerge for a decade offers little short-term commercial return compared to chronic medications sold daily in wealthy markets. Consequently, promising candidate drugs frequently stall in early clinical trials for lack of funding. Furthermore, when an outbreak strikes, hospital systems optimized for lean, just-in-time efficiency lack surge capacity in intensive-care beds, protective equipment, and trained nursing staff, causing preventable mortality even in affluent countries.",
"Journalist: How do global inequalities and public trust affect our ability to contain emerging diseases?",
"Dr. Tariq Al-Mansoor: Pathogens respect neither national borders nor patent laws. During recent global health emergencies, vaccine nationalism and intellectual-property bottlenecks left low-income countries waiting months or years for vital doses—a moral failure that also prolonged the pandemic by allowing new viral variants to evolve unchecked. Simultaneously, medical breakthroughs cannot succeed without public trust: vaccine hesitancy fueled by digital misinformation and inconsistent official communication can undermine even the most effective biomedical tools.",
"Journalist: What paradigm shift is needed to overcome these limitations?",
"Dr. Tariq Al-Mansoor: We must embrace a 'One Health' strategy that recognizes human health as inseparable from animal and ecosystem health—curbing habitat destruction, strengthening global pathogen surveillance, maintaining permanent public funding for preemptive vaccine libraries, and expanding regional manufacturing capacity across the Global South so that lifesaving medicines are treated as global public goods."
