      "Patricia Nkomo: 'Because political and financial systems operate on radically different time horizons from Earth's climate system. Electoral cycles last four or five years, and corporate quarterly reports reward immediate shareholder returns, whereas the most severe costs of climate inaction accumulate over decades. In the short term, decarbonizing steel, cement, aviation, and agriculture requires substantial upfront capital expenditure and disrupts established fossil-fuel industries and regions. Without credible transition plans for affected workers and communities, resistance to climate regulation is economically rational from a narrow short-term perspective.'",
      "Dr. Lucas Lindqvist: 'That short-termism is precisely what makes conventional cost-benefit accounting so misleading. Standard economic models routinely treat the cost of climate policies as an immediate burden while discounting or underestimating the staggering economic damages of inaction—destroyed coastal infrastructure, supply-chain breakdowns, plummeting labor productivity during extreme heatwaves, and escalating disaster relief bills. In truth, the choice is no longer between costly climate action and inexpensive business-as-usual; it is between planned, investment-led transformation today and chaotic, far costlier economic contraction tomorrow.'",
      "Patricia Nkomo: 'I agree that inaction is ruinously expensive, yet we must also recognize the distributional asymmetries across nations and social classes. Industrialized countries built their historical wealth on centuries of unrestricted carbon emissions, whereas emerging economies still need affordable energy to lift millions out of poverty. Similarly, within wealthy nations, flat carbon taxes without rebates disproportionately burden rural commuters and low-income households who spend a larger share of their income on heating and transport.'",
