"titre2": "Exercise 34",
"texte": [
"Public Speech — Ladies and gentlemen, delegates, fellow citizens, from the timber and fertile soils that sustained ancient agrarian empires to the coal, petroleum, rare minerals, and freshwater aquifers powering twenty-first-century civilization, natural resources have always formed the material bedrock of human societies. Yet for much of modern industrial history, dominant economic doctrines treated nature as an inexhaustible warehouse of 'free gifts' awaiting limitless extraction. Today, confronted with depleted fisheries, eroding topsoil, shrinking groundwater reserves, and polluted watersheds, humanity is compelled to confront a profound ethical and political imperative: replacing extractive predation with collective responsibility.",
"The tragedy of unrestrained resource exploitation stems from a fundamental misalignment between private short-term gain and shared long-term survival. When extraction rights are granted without strict ecological limits, corporations and states can capture immediate financial revenues while externalizing the true costs—poisoned rivers, degraded landscapes, lost biodiversity, and destabilized climates—onto local communities and future generations. Worse still, resource-rich regions in the developing world have frequently suffered the paradox of plenty: vast mineral or hydrocarbon wealth accompanied by environmental devastation, social conflict, and weak public services, while finished goods and corporate profits flow predominantly toward distant consumer markets.",
"Overcoming this extractive logic requires recognizing that critical natural resources are not mere commercial commodities, but shared ecological commons upon which all life depends. Even resources located within sovereign national borders—such as major tropical rainforests, transboundary river basins, and global mineral reserves—perform planetary functions that transcend political maps. Collective responsibility therefore operates on two inseparable levels: domestic accountability to protect citizens' environmental rights, and international solidarity to ensure that global consumption patterns do not export ecological ruin onto vulnerable regions.",
"In practical terms, translating collective responsibility into policy demands bold structural reforms across production and consumption. Governments must enforce rigorous environmental impact assessments, require free, prior, and informed consent from Indigenous and local communities affected by extraction, and mandate full corporate accountability across global supply chains. Simultaneously, industrial economies must transition from a linear 'take-make-dispose' model toward circular resource stewardship—designing durable, repairable products, taxing virgin resource depletion rather than human labor, and investing in ecosystem restoration.",
"Ultimately, how humanity governs Earth's finite resources is a defining test of moral maturity. True prosperity does not consist in liquidating our natural inheritance for fleeting material abundance, but in acting as conscientious trustees who preserve the vitality, beauty, and generosity of the biosphere for all who share the planet today and all who will inherit it tomorrow."
