"titre2": "Exercise 39",
"texte": [
"Public Speech — Ladies and gentlemen, fellow citizens, walk through any commercial district or scroll through any digital marketplace in an affluent society today, and you will encounter an unprecedented spectacle of material abundance. Within hours, garments sewn across oceans, electronics assembled from rare minerals, and out-of-season foods flown across hemispheres can arrive at our doorsteps at remarkably low retail prices. Yet behind this frictionless convenience lies an ecological and ethical ledger rarely visible at checkout: the richest ten percent of the world's population accounts for nearly half of global consumption-based carbon emissions, driving resource depletion, plastic pollution, and precarious labor conditions worldwide. Confronting our ecological crisis requires examining responsible consumption not as a peripheral lifestyle trend, but as a central question of civic ethics.",
"For decades, consumer culture has equated personal fulfillment and social status with continuous acquisition—an ethos reinforced by planned obsolescence, fast fashion, and algorithmic advertising engineered to manufacture perpetual dissatisfaction. Products are deliberately designed with glued batteries, unrepairable components, and fleeting aesthetic trends so that functional items are discarded long before their physical utility expires. This throwaway economy converts finite planetary resources into mountains of electronic and textile waste at an unsustainable pace.",
"At the same time, advocates of responsible consumption must avoid a common analytical trap: reducing systemic ecological transformation solely to individual shopping choices. Telling citizens to 'buy green' while leaving predatory corporate production models, fossil-fuel subsidies, and misleading eco-labels intact places an unfair moral burden on individual consumers—especially lower-income households who often cannot afford higher-priced organic or durable goods. Individual virtue cannot compensate for structural rules that make destructive products artificially cheap and sustainable alternatives prohibitively expensive.",
"Genuine responsible consumption therefore operates at the intersection of personal discernment and collective regulation. On an individual level, practicing 'sufficiency'—repairing existing possessions, sharing tools and mobility, prioritizing durability over novelty, and questioning manufactured desires—reclaims time, financial freedom, and authentic well-being from the treadmill of hyper-consumption. On a systemic level, citizens must demand ambitious public policies: enforceable 'right-to-repair' laws, mandatory product durability standards, bans on deceptive green marketing, and fair pricing that incorporates environmental costs.",
"Ultimately, embracing responsible consumption is not an exercise in joyless self-denial; it is an invitation to redefine what constitutes a good life. By shifting our aspirations from endless material accumulation toward durability, shared community resources, cultural richness, and ecological stewardship, affluent societies can live lighter upon the Earth while discovering deeper, more enduring forms of human flourishing."
