    "texte": [
      "Analytical Essay — Only a few decades ago, political theorists widely assumed that constitutional democracy had achieved irreversible historical ascendancy, destined to consolidate steadily across the globe. Today, that confidence has been shaken by a persistent wave of populist mobilization challenging established democratic norms from within. Across continents, charismatic leaders claiming to embody the sole authentic voice of 'the pure people' against a 'corrupt elite' have won majorities, reshaped public discourse, and tested institutional guardrails. Understanding this phenomenon requires moving beyond simplistic moral condemnation to examine both the structural grievances that fuel populist movements and the subtle mechanisms through which they erode liberal democracy.",
      "At its root, populism is less a coherent economic doctrine than a political logic built on moral polarization. Unlike pluralist democracy—which views society as composed of diverse citizens with legitimate, competing interests requiring negotiation and compromise—populism divides the social body into two irreconcilable camps. Once a leader claims exclusive moral legitimacy as the singular representative of the popular will, political opponents cease to be legitimate rivals and are recast as traitors to the nation. Consequently, constitutional checks and balances—independent courts, free press, electoral commissions, and minority protections—are portrayed not as safeguards of liberty, but as illegitimate obstacles thwarting the majority.",
      "Yet the resurgence of populism cannot be explained merely by demagogic manipulation; it is deeply rooted in unaddressed economic, cultural, and institutional failures. Decades of widening wealth inequality, deindustrialization in heartland regions, and precarious employment have left millions feeling abandoned by mainstream governing parties that converged around technocratic market orthodoxy. Concurrently, rapid cultural shifts and perceived loss of national control under globalization have generated status anxiety among groups who feel their ways of life are condescended to by metropolitan elites. When established parties fail to offer credible avenues for economic security and civic dignity, populist movements step into the vacuum.",
