      "Journalistic Column — In any functioning democracy, the news media occupy a vital constitutional space between elected authorities and the citizenry. Far more than passive mirrors reflecting objective events, journalism actively constructs the public agenda—determining which social problems command urgent attention, how political conflicts are framed, and whose voices are granted credibility in national debate. When healthy, independent media hold power to account, expose systemic abuses, and supply the shared factual foundation required for informed self-government. Yet over the past two decades, profound economic and technological disruptions have transformed how media operate, raising urgent questions about their evolving influence over public opinion.",
      "Media scholars have long emphasized that news organizations shape public consciousness not merely by telling audiences what to think, but through 'agenda-setting' and 'framing.' By prioritizing certain stories on front pages or prime-time broadcasts while relegating others to obscurity, editors establish a hierarchy of public concern. Simultaneously, the linguistic metaphors and narrative frames chosen to describe complex issues—portraying migration either as a humanitarian duty or a security invasion, or framing public investment either as vital infrastructure or reckless spending—powerfully steer emotional reactions and policy preferences before citizens even weigh empirical evidence.",
