"titre": "Preserving Cultural Heritage in the Age of Globalisation",
"titre2": "Exercise 53",
"texte": [
"Narrative & Literary Reflection — In the narrow stone alleys of Fez's ancient medina, master zellij tilemaker Hassan Benjelloun guides a young apprentice's hand as he chips geometric stars from glazed terracotta with a hand-forged hammer. Just outside the city walls, smartphone billboards advertise global streaming platforms, fast-food franchises, and international fashion chains identical to those lining boulevards in Madrid, Seoul, or Toronto. This juxtaposition captures one of the defining cultural dilemmas of our era: how can communities preserve distinctive tangible and intangible heritage amid the homogenizing currents of economic and digital globalization?",
"For centuries, cultural heritage was conceived primarily in monumental, static terms—ancient temples, royal palaces, and museum artifacts curated behind glass as relics of a finished past. Over recent decades, however, anthropologists and international conventions have broadened that understanding to encompass living intangible heritage: oral epics, musical traditions, culinary arts, vernacular architecture, ecological farming practices, and artisanal craftsmanship transmitted from master to apprentice. Far from being mere nostalgic ornaments, these living traditions anchor collective memory, foster aesthetic creativity, and provide communities with a resilient sense of belonging in a rapidly shifting world.",
"Yet globalization subjects cultural heritage to intense, contradictory pressures. On one hand, transnational media ecosystems and consumer uniformity threaten to marginalize local languages, music, and crafts, persuading younger generations that modernity requires imitating dominant global styles. On the other hand, mass international tourism and commercial heritage branding often transform historic neighborhoods into sanitized 'open-air museums.' When soaring property rents driven by short-term vacation rentals force local residents and artisans out of historic centers, architectural facades may be preserved while the living community that gave them meaning is hollowed out.",
"Equally dangerous is the temptation to defend heritage through rigid cultural purism that treats tradition as an unchanging museum piece sealed off from external influence. Historically, the richest cultural traditions—from Mediterranean cuisine to West African rhythms and Silk Road textiles—flourished precisely through creative dialogue, migration, and adaptation across cultures. Freezing heritage in nostalgic orthodoxy strips it of the vitality needed to inspire new generations.",
"Preserving cultural heritage in the age of globalization therefore requires cultivating 'living continuity' rather than static preservation. Investing in vocational apprenticeships for traditional crafts, regulating overtourism to keep historic districts habitable for working residents, utilizing digital archives and global platforms to share local arts on communities' own terms, and encouraging contemporary artists to reinterpret ancestral forms ensure that heritage remains a dynamic bridge between memory and future invention."
