"Dr. Sofia Lindholm: 'Because neuroimaging and behavioral studies demonstrate that managing multiple languages acts as a continuous, natural workout for the brain's executive control network. When a multilingual person speaks one language, the other languages remain subtly active in the background, requiring the brain to select, inhibit, and switch seamlessly between linguistic systems. Over a lifetime, this mental agility strengthens attentional control, cognitive flexibility, metalinguistic awareness, and problem-solving—and research even indicates that lifelong bilingualism can delay the onset of age-related cognitive decline by several years.'",
"Dr. Kwame Mensah: 'Beyond individual neurology, the social and pedagogical implications are equally transformative, particularly in multilingual regions across Africa, Asia, and increasingly diverse global cities. When school systems force children to abandon their home languages upon entering the classroom, they squander a rich cognitive foundation and alienate families from schooling. Conversely, structured mother-tongue-based multilingual education—where children build foundational literacy in their first language while progressively mastering national and international languages—consistently yields higher academic achievement across all subjects.'",
"Dr. Sofia Lindholm: 'I completely agree, and multilingualism also cultivates vital intercultural empathy. Learning another language is never merely memorizing vocabulary lists and grammatical rules; it is entering another community's conceptual universe, humor, idioms, and historical sensibilities. In an interconnected yet polarized world, individuals capable of navigating multiple linguistic perspectives serve as indispensable cultural mediators who challenge stereotyping and foster dialogue across divides.'",
"Dr. Kwame Mensah: 'Yet a troubling double standard still persists in many societies: elite bilingualism—such as affluent students learning English, French, or Mandarin—is celebrated as prestigious sophistication, whereas the multilingualism of immigrant or indigenous children speaking minority tongues is too often treated as an educational deficit.'",
"Dr. Sofia Lindholm: 'Dismantling that hierarchy is essential. Every language a child brings into the classroom is an intellectual treasure. By training teachers in multilingual pedagogy, developing quality learning materials in local languages, and valuing linguistic diversity as a shared public wealth, societies can unlock both individual brilliance and deeper social cohesion.'"
