"titre2": "Exercise 58",
"texte": [
"Public Speech — Ladies and gentlemen, university rectors, scientists, students, across higher education and public policy today, a powerful orthodoxy insists that national competitiveness and individual employability depend almost exclusively on science, technology, engineering, and mathematics. Faced with rapid advances in artificial intelligence, biotechnology, and green engineering, governments and donors routinely channel funding toward technical laboratories while treating literature, history, philosophy, languages, and the arts as ornamental luxuries—pleasant for personal enrichment, yet dispensable when budgets tighten. That hierarchy rests on a profound misunderstanding of what a technological civilization actually requires to survive and flourish.",
"Far from being obsolete in an age of smart machines, the humanities cultivate precisely the capacities that algorithms cannot replicate and that technological power makes most urgent. Science and engineering teach us how to manipulate matter, optimize systems, and build powerful tools; the humanities ask what ends those tools ought to serve, what constitutes a dignified human life, and how power should be constrained by justice. When an autonomous vehicle must weigh competing risks, when a biomedical firm edits human genes, or when a social platform designs attention-capturing algorithms, the decisive questions are not merely technical—they are profoundly ethical, historical, and philosophical.",
"Moreover, the humanities train habits of mind essential for navigating complexity and ambiguity. Reading a nineteenth-century novel, interpreting contested historical archives, or dissecting a philosophical argument teaches students to recognize unspoken assumptions, appreciate cultural and historical context, empathize with lives unlike their own, and communicate persuasively across differences. In workplaces increasingly automated for routine coding and data processing, employers consistently discover that long-term leadership requires ethical judgment, intercultural fluency, narrative clarity, and creative synthesis—capacities honed at the heart of humanistic inquiry.",
"Equally vital is the civic role of the humanities in sustaining democratic self-government. Democracies do not falter merely from technical inefficiency; they decay when citizens lose historical memory, succumb to simplistic propaganda, and cease to recognize the shared humanity of political opponents. By equipping citizens to interrogate dominant narratives and remember past tragedies and triumphs, history, literature, and philosophy act as indispensable immune systems against authoritarian manipulation.",
"Defending the humanities therefore does not mean pitting them against science and engineering in a sterile academic rivalry. Rather, the twenty-first century demands a deep reunification of human knowledge—integrating ethics, history, and social inquiry directly into scientific and technical education, while ensuring that public universities keep the humanities accessible to students of every socioeconomic background. Only by uniting technical ingenuity with humanistic wisdom can humanity ensure that its extraordinary tools remain servants of human dignity."
