"titre": "Universal Access to Knowledge: Ideal or Reality?",
"titre2": "Exercise 59",
"texte": [
"Journalistic Column — When the pioneers of the World Wide Web envisioned a decentralized global network three decades ago, their guiding dream was exhilaratingly democratic: every human being, regardless of geography or wealth, would gain instantaneous access to the totality of scientific, literary, and historical knowledge. Today, open-access encyclopedias, digitized museum collections, massive open online courses, and preprint scientific servers have partially fulfilled that promise, enabling a student in a provincial town to consult lectures and texts once locked inside elite university libraries. Yet beneath this celebratory narrative lies a stubborn reality: universal access to knowledge remains sharply stratified by economic, linguistic, and infrastructural barriers.",
"The first glaring obstacle is the persistent global digital divide. According to international telecommunications assessments, roughly a third of humanity—nearly three billion people—remains offline, while hundreds of millions more rely on slow, prohibitively expensive mobile connections that make downloading academic papers or streaming educational videos impossible. Within both developing nations and rural pockets of wealthy countries, disparities in broadband infrastructure, reliable electricity, and device ownership ensure that the information superhighway still bypasses the poorest households.",
"Even among connected populations, critical scholarly knowledge is frequently enclosed behind steep commercial paywalls. In academic publishing, a handful of giant commercial conglomerates reap extraordinary profit margins by charging universities and readers exorbitant subscription fees to access publicly funded scientific research. While researchers write and peer-review articles without payment, institutions in the Global South—and independent scholars or citizens anywhere—are routinely locked out of cutting-edge medical, climate, and engineering discoveries unless they pay prohibitive per-article fees.",
"Equally exclusionary is the overwhelming linguistic and cultural concentration of online knowledge. More than half of all web content is produced in English, a language spoken by less than twenty percent of the world's population, while thousands of regional and indigenous languages possess minimal digital representation. When educational platforms, scientific journals, and artificial intelligence training datasets reflect predominantly Western, English-language perspectives, non-English speakers face not merely a translation hurdle, but an epistemic hierarchy that marginalizes local knowledge systems.",
"Transforming universal access to knowledge from an aspirational ideal into lived reality therefore requires concerted public policy and global solidarity. Expanding affordable rural broadband, mandating immediate open-access publication for all publicly funded research, supporting multilingual translation and open educational resources, and strengthening public libraries as community digital hubs are vital steps. Knowledge is a unique public good that multiplies rather than diminishes when shared; ensuring it belongs to all humanity is both a moral imperative and the surest investment in collective progress."
