"titre2": "Exercise 65",
"texte": [
"Correspondence & Testimonials — Oxford, 17 May — Dear Thomas, following our seminar on John Stuart Mill's On Liberty, I wanted to share two testimonies exploring how social conformity challenges freedom of thought today. In authoritarian regimes, censorship operates visibly through state police, banned books, and imprisoned dissidents. In open, pluralistic democracies, however, intellectual conformity assumes a far subtler guise: the quiet pressure of peer approval, professional orthodoxy, and digital crowd dynamics that make dissenting from prevailing consensus feel socially costly.",
"Testimony from Professor David Chen, 54, theoretical physicist and academic journal editor: 'Even within scientific communities dedicated to open inquiry, subtle groupthink can take hold around dominant paradigms. Younger researchers whose findings challenge fashionable hypotheses often hesitate to publish unconventional results for fear of losing grant funding, facing hostile peer reviews, or damaging their career prospects. Yet the entire history of science—from heliocentrism to plate tectonics—demonstrates that breakthroughs frequently begin as unpopular minority insights that initially unsettled established consensus.'",
"Continuation of letter to Thomas — David's reflection echoes Mill's classic warning against the 'tyranny of the majority'—a social atmosphere in which prevailing opinion polices thought not through legal penalties, but through ostracism and reputational anxiety. In the digital age, online shaming and instant pile-ons have dramatically amplified that social pressure.",
"Testimony from Maya Al-Jamil, 27, documentary producer and community organizer: 'On social media platforms, nuanced opinions rarely survive the demand for immediate ideological alignment. If you express partial agreement with an opposing viewpoint or question a popular slogan within your own circle, you risk swift moral condemnation and social exclusion. Over time, many thoughtful people simply retreat into self-censorship—privately harboring doubts while publicly echoing safe orthodoxies. When everyone performs conformity out of fear of isolation, society loses the honest friction through which errors are corrected and genuine conviction is forged.'",
"Closing of letter to Thomas — As both David and Maya illustrate, legal guarantees of free speech remain hollow unless sustained by a courageous civic culture that values intellectual independence. Protecting freedom of thought requires cultivating educational spaces that reward respectful dissent, safeguarding academic and journalistic autonomy, and practicing the personal integrity to listen charitably and speak honestly even when standing apart from the crowd."
