"titre": "Individual Responsibility in the Face of Collective Injustice",
"titre2": "Exercise 69",
"texte": [
"Public Speech — Ladies and gentlemen, human-rights advocates, students, fellow citizens, when we confront vast systemic injustices—institutional discrimination, environmental destruction, exploitative global supply chains, or authoritarian abuses—a paralyzing question inevitably arises: what moral responsibility bears a single ordinary individual amid wrongs orchestrated by massive structures far larger than oneself? Faced with the sheer scale of collective injustice, two tempting yet morally corrosive excuses frequently emerge: fatalistic helplessness ('I am only one person; nothing I do can change the system') and self-exonerating conformity ('Everyone else participates; I am merely following rules and fulfilling my role').",
"Twentieth-century moral philosophy, forged in the crucible of totalitarian catastrophes, dismantled both of those alibis with uncompromising clarity. Thinkers such as Hannah Arendt and Karl Jaspers demonstrated that systemic evil rarely relies solely on fanatical monsters; far more often, it depends on the quiet, unthinking compliance of millions of ordinary citizens who refuse to judge independently, compartmentalize their conscience, and treat obedience as a substitute for moral agency. When individuals abdicate personal judgment inside bureaucracies, corporations, or markets, collective injustice acquires unstoppable momentum.",
"At the same time, recognizing individual moral responsibility does not mean pretending that isolated personal gestures can single-handedly dismantle entrenched structural injustices. Buying fair-trade coffee or recycling household waste, while ethically meaningful, cannot by itself reform labor treaties or decarbonize global industry. Indeed, framing systemic problems solely as tests of private lifestyle purity can induce moral exhaustion while leaving institutional power untouched. True individual responsibility lies not in heroic omnipotence, but in 'political and civic co-responsibility'—joining with others to challenge unjust rules, support accountability, and build fairer institutions.",
"Concretely, this responsibility manifests across multiple spheres of everyday life. In our workplaces and professions, it means refusing complicity in deceptive or harmful practices and protecting whistleblowers; in our economic choices, it means questioning the human and ecological origins of what we consume; and in our civic lives, it means voting, organizing, speaking out when minorities are scapegoated, and defending the rule of law even when silence is comfortable.",
"Ultimately, history is never shaped solely by impersonal forces; it is steered by countless individual decisions either to acquiesce or to resist. While no single person can repair every injustice in the world, every person remains accountable for where they stand, what they normalize, and how they act alongside their fellow citizens in defense of human dignity."
],
