      "Journalistic Column — Although the World Health Organization's 1946 constitution proclaimed the highest attainable standard of health as a fundamental human right without distinction of race, religion, political belief, or economic condition, a child's life expectancy and survival prospects remain heavily dictated by the lottery of birth. According to global health data, more than four billion people—over half of humanity—still lack full coverage of essential health services, while roughly one hundred million individuals are pushed into extreme poverty every year by out-of-pocket medical expenses. Beneath soaring headlines celebrating precision gene therapies and robotic surgery lies a stark global divide between those who enjoy cutting-edge care and millions denied basic primary medicine.",
      "This healthcare chasm operates both between nations and within them. Across low-income countries, chronic underfunding of public health systems, dilapidated rural clinics, unreliable electricity and clean water, and acute shortages of trained physicians, nurses, and midwives leave preventable conditions—such as maternal complications, malaria, tuberculosis, and childhood pneumonia—deadly. Compounding this vulnerability is the global 'brain drain' of medical personnel: wealthy nations facing aging populations routinely recruit doctors and nurses trained at public expense in developing economies, depleting fragile health systems of their most vital human capital.",
      "Simultaneously, profound health disparities persist inside affluent industrialized societies, where zip code often predicts longevity more accurately than genetic code. Low-income neighborhoods and marginalized racial or rural communities frequently suffer higher rates of chronic illness, shorter life expectancy, and reduced access to preventive screenings and specialist care. Even in countries with nominal universal coverage, hidden barriers—such as high co-payments, lengthy waiting lists, linguistic obstacles, and closure of rural hospitals—create a two-tier reality in which wealthy citizens purchase rapid private treatment while working families endure delayed diagnoses.",
