"Correspondence & Testimonials — Boston, 12 June — Dear Beatrice, following our policy forum on health economics, I wanted to share two testimonies from frontline practitioners illustrating how the growing commercialization of healthcare reshapes clinical care and social equity. Over the past several decades, market-oriented reforms—ranging from hospital privatization and private equity acquisitions of clinics to profit-driven insurance networks—have increasingly treated medical care less as a public service governed by solidarity than as a lucrative commodity governed by return on investment.",
"Testimony from Dr. Marcus Reid, 46, emergency department physician at an urban community hospital: 'When a hospital system is acquired by profit-maximizing investors, the subtle shift in clinical priorities is unmistakable. Lucrative elective procedures—such as cosmetic orthopedics or specialized imaging—are aggressively expanded, while essential yet less profitable services like psychiatric wards, maternity units, and rural emergency departments are scaled back or shuttered. Physicians face mounting pressure to shorten patient consultations to fifteen-minute slots and meet billing targets, eroding the unhurried listening and diagnostic thoroughness that safe medicine demands.'",
"Continuation of letter to Beatrice — Dr. Reid's experience highlights a fundamental economic flaw identified long ago by Nobel laureate Kenneth Arrow: healthcare does not behave like an ordinary consumer market. Patients experiencing acute chest pain or a cancer diagnosis cannot shop around leisurely for price comparisons, and asymmetric medical knowledge leaves them reliant on fiduciary professional trust.",
"Testimony from Sofia Morales, 39, oncology patient navigator and social worker: 'Every week I counsel working families devastated by financial toxicity—patients delaying chemotherapy or skipping prescribed medications because of soaring insurance deductibles, surprise out-of-network bills, and aggressive debt collection. Meanwhile, administrative bureaucracy consumes a massive share of healthcare spending as insurers and providers employ armies of billing specialists to contest claims. No citizen should ever be forced to choose between bankruptcy and survival when illness strikes.'",
"Closing of letter to Beatrice — As both Marcus and Sofia demonstrate, subordinating healthcare to commercial profit inflates administrative waste, distorts medical priorities, and deepens social inequality. Preserving humane, equitable medicine requires anchoring health systems in universal public financing, strict regulation of private-equity ownership in clinical care, transparent pricing, and the ethical principle that medical need—never ability to pay—must govern access to healing."
