      "Analytical Essay — Across East Asia, Europe, North America, and increasingly across Latin America and parts of the Middle East, humanity is experiencing one of the most profound demographic shifts in its history: rapid population ageing. Driven by falling fertility rates alongside remarkable gains in life expectancy, the proportion of citizens aged sixty-five and older is expanding at an unprecedented pace. While longer human lifespans represent an extraordinary triumph of sanitation, nutrition, and modern medicine, demographic ageing confronts twentieth-century healthcare systems with a structural challenge they were never designed to meet.",
      "Most contemporary health systems were built around an episodic, acute-care paradigm—engineered to diagnose and cure isolated infections, injuries, or sudden surgical emergencies in relatively young populations. Older adults, by contrast, frequently live with multiple interacting chronic conditions—cardiovascular disease, diabetes, osteoarthritis, sensory impairment, and neurodegenerative disorders such as dementia. Treating multimorbidity through fragmented specialist silos often leads to uncoordinated prescriptions, adverse drug interactions, and repeated emergency hospitalizations that diminish quality of life while straining public budgets.",
      "Simultaneously, demographic ageing exposes a severe shortage in long-term care infrastructure and geriatric workforce capacity. Across wealthy and middle-income societies alike, nursing homes and home-care agencies face chronic staffing deficits because eldercare remains underpaid, physically demanding, and socially undervalued. Historically, families—and disproportionately women—absorbed unpaid caregiving responsibilities; yet smaller family sizes, geographic mobility, and dual-earner households mean informal kinship care can no longer carry this burden alone without comprehensive public support.",
