"Journalistic Column — Less than a century ago, a simple scratch from a rose thorn, a routine dental extraction, or a mild bout of pneumonia could prove fatal. The discovery of penicillin and subsequent classes of antibiotics revolutionized modern medicine, turning once-lethal bacterial infections into easily curable ailments while making complex surgeries, organ transplants, and cancer chemotherapy safely possible. Today, however, that medical bedrock is quietly crumbling under the relentless advance of antimicrobial resistance (AMR)—a biological phenomenon in which bacteria evolve the ability to survive drugs designed to kill them. Public health experts increasingly warn that without decisive global action, humanity risks sliding into a 'post-antibiotic era' where common infections once again claim millions of lives annually.",
"Evolutionary biologists note that microbial resistance is a natural adaptive process, yet human practices have accelerated it to catastrophic speed. In healthcare systems worldwide, overprescription of broad-spectrum antibiotics for viral illnesses such as colds or influenza—against which antibiotics are completely ineffective—exposes bacterial populations to unnecessary selective pressure. Simultaneously, in many countries where antimicrobials are sold over the counter without medical supervision, patients frequently take incomplete courses, allowing the hardiest resistant microbes to survive and multiply.",
"Even more consequential is the massive use of antibiotics outside human medicine. Roughly seventy percent of all medically important antibiotics sold globally are administered not to sick people, but to livestock, poultry, and farmed fish—often not to treat acute illness, but to promote faster weight gain and compensate for overcrowded, unsanitary industrial farming conditions. Resistant bacteria and resistance genes subsequently spread from farms into soils, waterways, and food chains, creating a global ecological reservoir of drug-resistant pathogens.",
"Compounding this crisis is a severe market failure in pharmaceutical innovation. Developing a genuinely novel class of antibiotics is scientifically arduous and costly, yet commercial returns are low because any new antibiotic must be used sparingly as a drug of last resort to preserve its effectiveness. Consequently, most major pharmaceutical corporations have abandoned antibiotic research in favor of lucrative daily medications for chronic conditions, leaving the global drug pipeline dangerously thin.",
