      "Journalistic Column — Anyone walking through vibrant metropolises such as Lagos, Kinshasa, Addis Ababa, or Abidjan today can witness one of the most dynamic social phenomena of our time at close range: the rapid urbanization of the African continent. No other world region is currently experiencing comparable urban growth. While only a small proportion of Africa's population lived in cities around the middle of the twentieth century, more than half of the continent's population is expected to live in urban areas by the middle of this century. This rapid urban development is both a driving force behind economic innovation and an enormous challenge for infrastructure, the environment, and social cohesion.",
      "On the one hand, African major cities function as powerful centers of creativity, trade, culture, and social mobility. Dense urban networks support thriving service sectors, cultural industries, technology hubs, and educational opportunities that attract young people from rural areas. Cities facilitate the division of labor, business networking, and easier access to specialized markets, enabling them to make a disproportionately large contribution to national economic output.",
      "On the other hand, the pace of migration into many cities far exceeds the planning and financial capacities of local authorities. Unlike Europe's historical industrialization, urbanization in many African countries is not accompanied by a corresponding expansion of manufacturing industries and formal employment opportunities. As a result, a significant proportion of urban development takes place informally: extensive residential neighborhoods emerge without proper urban planning, sewage systems, safe drinking water supplies, waste collection, or reliable electricity networks. Congested roads cost commuters valuable time every day and significantly reduce economic productivity.",
      "At the same time, climate change is dramatically increasing the vulnerability of densely populated urban areas. Many of the fastest-growing metropolises are located along low-lying coastlines or major river systems. Heavy rainfall, flooding, coastal erosion, and urban heat islands disproportionately affect low-income residents of informal settlements, whose homes provide little protection against natural disasters.",
      "For African cities to become livable, inclusive, and climate-resilient places for the future, a forward-looking change in urban policy is essential. Local governments need their own taxation powers and reliable financial resources to invest in affordable social housing, efficient public transportation systems, green infrastructure, and decentralized water and energy supplies. If urbanization is managed proactively and inclusively, it can become a decisive driver of sustainable prosperity across the entire continent."
