      "Journalistic Column — In an era saturated with competing news channels, partisan commentary, viral eyewitness clips, and synthetic media, ordinary citizens frequently experience a disorienting epistemological vertigo: faced with fiercely contradictory accounts of the very same political event, scientific finding, or geopolitical crisis, how can anyone discern what is genuinely true? Whereas earlier generations often struggled against scarcity of information or state censorship, contemporary societies confront an opposite predicament—an overwhelming flood of rival narratives engineered to confirm tribal loyalties and erode confidence in shared reality.",
      "Understanding why truth becomes so contested requires distinguishing between legitimate perspectival interpretation and deliberate factual distortion. Complex historical and social events inevitably admit multiple analytical lenses: an economic reform, for example, may appear simultaneously as an efficiency triumph to investors and a social hardship to displaced workers. Pluralistic democracy thrives on debating such differing interpretations openly. Yet democratic deliberation collapses when empirical facts themselves—whether a vaccine reduces mortality, whether ballots were lawfully counted, or whether greenhouse gases warm the atmosphere—are treated as mere matters of partisan opinion.",
